Review mode: read-only acceptance gate on the builder's behalf. Review moment: 2026-09-20. The full `epics.md`, PRD, architecture spine, D1–D19 addendum, and binding Q1–Q5 ruling were read.

Evidence boundary: `epics.md` SHA-256 `77fac3369c99a2ae14195c19ac467a72aeff146fd8ced606511463f6131f7a83`; PRD `65cfaaa3fb906c8bc2ce095ab09ce9f648e962c3e964c8d7bc9ac8ad69db3c1b`; spine `a54481da20a3068d4ccd3e95af3410830503446a3a39d60533b7d67cf9185a0a`; addendum `8995d2163653736479874a0d19945e2ce587d6e98d9083ba60acb590d9e5e847`; ruling `c3ec3841dae9b304c02d8cb26406bcf8e8a951b40ca42217e43e76a69829d977`.

## Gate verdict

**NOT SAFE to hand to development agents yet.** The story template is mechanically complete, the main vertical checkpoints are present, and several safety/release gates are strong. The breakdown still has ordering blockers, frozen-contract omissions, unresolved decisions, and release-proof gaps. Every epic is `fix-first`; none is `safe` as written.

The static scan found exactly 33 stories. Every story has an `As ... / I want ... / So that ...` statement and at least one `Given / When / Then` acceptance clause. That proves shape only. It does not prove that each clause is independently executable or that the story can be completed without a later story.

The required bundled ACP 3-model gate was not run in this lane. `review-gate.mjs` requires a static packet file, while the request restricts review work to read-only and the only required write is the flat outbox. The round-table below is therefore an inline five-voice critique, not an independently dispatched three-model approval.

